To see the **Iterator pattern** in action, let's look at a simple implementation for a "Notification System." This allows us to loop through a list of messages without the main code needing to know if the messages are stored in an array, a list, or a set.
### 1. The Components
First, we define what a **Notification** is and create the **Iterator** that will handle the movement.
```java
// The Item
class Notification {
    String message;
    public Notification(String message) { this.message = message; }
    public String getMessage() { return message; }
}

// The Collection (Aggregate)
class NotificationCollection {
    private List<Notification> notifications = new ArrayList<>();

    public void addNotification(String str) {
        notifications.add(new Notification(str));
    }

    // This creates and returns the "Remote Control"
    public Iterator<Notification> createIterator() {
        return new NotificationIterator(notifications);
    }
}

```
### 2. The Iterator Implementation
This is the "Brain" that knows how to move through the data. It tracks the current position (pos).
```java
class NotificationIterator implements Iterator<Notification> {
    private List<Notification> notifications;
    private int pos = 0;

    public NotificationIterator(List<Notification> notifications) {
        this.notifications = notifications;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean hasNext() {
        // Check if we have reached the end
        return pos < notifications.size();
    }

    @Override
    public Notification next() {
        // Return current item and move to next
        return notifications.get(pos++);
    }
}

```
### 3. Traversing the Collection
Now, look at how the **Client Code** uses it. Notice that the main method never touches the ArrayList directly. It only talks to the Iterator.
```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NotificationCollection collection = new NotificationCollection();
        collection.addNotification("New Bid Placed!");
        collection.addNotification("Outbid Alert!");
        collection.addNotification("Auction Closing Soon!");

        // Get the iterator
        Iterator<Notification> iterator = collection.createIterator();

        System.out.println("--- Notification History ---");
        
        // Use the iterator to traverse
        while (iterator.hasNext()) {
            Notification n = iterator.next();
            System.out.println("Alert: " + n.getMessage());
        }
    }
}

```
### Why this is "Expert" Code
 1. **Decoupling:** If tomorrow you change the ArrayList inside NotificationCollection to a LinkedList or a custom BinaryTree for performance, the main method **never has to change.**
 2. **Safety:** You can pass the iterator to a different part of the system (like a UI component) and that component can read the data, but it can't accidentally add() or remove() items from your original list.
 3. **Multiple Passes:** You could create two different iterators and have them both at different positions in the same list at the same time.
### 💡 A Note on Java's Built-in Support
In your professional work with **Spring Boot** or **Java 8+**, you almost never write the while(hasNext) loop anymore. You use the "Enhanced for-loop" or "Streams":
```java
// Java does the Iterator work for you behind the scenes here
for (Notification n : collection) { 
    System.out.println(n.getMessage());
}

```
